@prefix : <http://www.semanticweb.org/edeyson/ontologies/2024/CCO#>.
@prefix gufo: <http://purl.org/nemo/gufo#>.
@prefix rdf: <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#>.
@prefix rdfs: <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#>.
@prefix owl: <http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#>.
@prefix xsd: <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#>.

<http://www.semanticweb.org/edeyson/ontologies/2024/CCO> rdf:type owl:Ontology;
    owl:imports gufo:.
:AtomicCompetence rdf:type owl:Class, gufo:SubKind, owl:NamedIndividual;
    rdfs:label "AtomicCompetence"@en;
    rdfs:comment "A singular (atomic) competency specification might address the goals for a solitary job function or curricular element constructed from a suitable competency statement and K, S, and D components."@en.
:ComposedCompetence rdf:type owl:Class, gufo:SubKind, owl:NamedIndividual;
    rdfs:label "ComposedCompetence"@en;
    rdfs:comment "Composite competency specifications unfold as tree structures with branch and leaf\nnodes. \n\nA composite competency specification (C) may combine (aggregate) both atomic (A) and other composite (C) competencies."@en.
:Disposition rdf:type owl:Class, gufo:Kind, owl:NamedIndividual;
    rdfs:subClassOf gufo:FunctionalComplex;
    rdfs:label "Disposition"@en;
    rdfs:comment "Dispositions, fundamental to the competency framework, encapsulate the \"know-why\" dimension, prescribing the character or quality requisite in task performance. They shape the discernment in skillful engagement of \"know-what\" (knowledge) and \"know-how\" (skills), acting as a filter through which decisions and actions are evaluated and chosen. Specific to the task context, dispositions exert a moderating or controlling influence on a practitioner's choices, suggesting or projecting a desirable quality onto outcomes, grounded in the professional values underlying the competency.\n\nFurthermore, dispositions not only reflect the professional values behind a competency but also characterize socio-emotional tendencies, predilections, and attitudes that define the inclination to carry out tasks and the sensitivity to know when and how to engage in those tasks. These characteristics include, but are not limited to, empathy, integrity, perseverance, openness to new ideas, commitment to excellence, ethical responsibility, and the ability to work effectively in collaborative and diverse environments.\n\nThe \"know-why\" component manifests as enacted values, and due to the challenge of directly assessing values and intentions, dispositions are typically evaluated indirectly. This is achieved through the observation of behavior patterns, reflective practice, and, in some cases, through self-reflective narratives that practitioners share about their experiences, decisions, and learnings. These assessments seek evidence of critical reflection, adaptability, moral and ethical judgment, and the ability to integrate personal and professional values in a cohesive manner.\n\nFor effective disposition assessment, it's crucial to foster environments that encourage critical reflection, questioning, and ethical debate, thereby facilitating the expression of dispositions in real and simulated contexts. Assessment methods may include case study analyses, simulations, reflective portfolios, peer and self-evaluations, providing valuable insights into how individuals internalize and demonstrate their professional values in action.\n\nIn summary, dispositions are crucial for the effective and ethical application of competencies in professional settings, acting as the glue that binds knowledge and skills with the fundamental values of a profession, ensuring that actions taken are not only efficient but also ethically sound and socially responsible."@en.
:Skill rdf:type owl:Class, gufo:Kind, owl:NamedIndividual;
    rdfs:subClassOf gufo:FunctionalComplex;
    rdfs:label "Skill"@en;
    rdfs:comment "Skills embody the capability and strategy for applying \"know-what\" knowledge effectively to perform tasks within specific contexts. The realization of competency hinges on the application of \"know-what\" through action�thereby, in practice, skills manifest as \"know-how\". This transformation from knowledge to action underscores the dynamic interplay between understanding and execution, where skills serve as the conduit for translating theoretical knowledge into practical outcomes.\n\nSkill acquisition is an iterative and often non-linear process, deeply rooted in practice and experiential learning. It extends beyond mere repetition or rote execution; it involves engaging with tasks at progressively higher levels of complexity and abstraction, thereby fostering a deeper understanding and mastery over time. This developmental journey aligns with the progressive hierarchy of cognitive processes, as articulated in Bloom�s Taxonomy, which ranges from basic recall of information to complex analytical, evaluative, and creative tasks. This progression underscores the evolution from novice to expert within a domain, characterized by an increasing sophistication in thought and application.\n\nSkills development is inherently linked to reflective practice�a deliberate process where individuals critically assess their actions and decisions to glean insights and improve future performance. This reflective cycle not only enhances skill proficiency but also fosters a mindset of continuous improvement and adaptability, crucial in rapidly evolving professional landscapes.\n\nThe assessment of skills often transcends direct observation of task execution, extending into the evaluation of the process and the quality of work produced. This involves a nuanced analysis of how effectively \"know-what\" is animated by \"know-how\", integrating both dimensions of competency. Therefore, the tangible impact of any knowledge element within a competency framework becomes fully appreciable only when it is manifested through a level of skillfulness. Such assessments frequently leverage Bloom�s Taxonomy to specify or observe competencies at various cognitive levels, facilitating a structured and comprehensive evaluation of skill proficiency.\n\nMoreover, the context in which skills are applied plays a pivotal role in defining their relevance and efficacy. Skills are not static capabilities but are deeply influenced by the situational nuances and challenges of the task at hand. This adaptability and contextual sensitivity are essential attributes of a skilled practitioner, highlighting the importance of not only mastering a skill but also understanding when, where, and how it is most effectively applied.\n\nIn essence, skills are the bridge between theoretical knowledge and practical execution, embodying the strategic application of \"know-what\" in real-world scenarios. They are cultivated through a deliberate practice and reflective learning process, evaluated through a combination of direct and indirect methods, and refined in response to the demands and complexities of specific contexts. Through this lens, skill development is seen as a cornerstone of competency, enabling individuals to navigate and excel in their professional and personal endeavors with agility and insight.\n\n"@en.
:Knowledge rdf:type owl:Class, gufo:Kind, owl:NamedIndividual;
    rdfs:subClassOf gufo:FunctionalComplex;
    rdfs:label "Knowledge"@en;
    rdfs:comment "Knowledge is the \"know-what\" dimension of competency that can be understood as factual. An element of knowledge designates a core concept essential to a competency.\n\nThis dimension reflects the enumerated subject matter that teachers catalog as topics in their syllabi, departments distribute and balance among the courses they develop in anacademic program, accreditation organizations stipulate in their accreditation criteria, and employers identify in job descriptions of their workers. \n\n"@en.
:KnowledgeSkill rdf:type owl:Class, gufo:Kind, owl:NamedIndividual;
    rdfs:subClassOf gufo:Relator;
    rdfs:label "KnowledgeSkill"@en;
    rdfs:comment "An element of knowledge and the level of skill with which it is applied are necessarily and naturally conjoined as paired in the specification of a competency."@en.
:CognitiveActionVerbs rdf:type owl:Class, gufo:Kind, owl:NamedIndividual;
    rdfs:subClassOf gufo:FunctionalComplex;
	rdfs:label "Cognitive Action Verbs"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "Verbs used to describe cognitive actions based on Bloom's Revised Taxonomy." .
:BloomCognitiveSkill rdf:type owl:Class, gufo:SubKind, owl:NamedIndividual;
    rdfs:label "Bloom's Cognitive Skill"@en;
    rdfs:comment "Levels of cognitive skills according to Bloom's Revised Taxonomy." .	
:Apply rdf:type owl:Class, gufo:SubKind, owl:NamedIndividual;
    rdfs:label "Apply"@en;
    rdfs:comment "Apply - carrying out or using a procedure in a given situation.\n\n- Executing, carrying out: applying a procedure to a familiar task. Executing is more frequently associated with the use of skills and algorithms than with techniques and methods.\n\n- Implementing, using: adjusting a process to a new situation. In the case of implementing, then, to understand conceptual knowledge is a prerequisite to being able to apply procedural knowledge.\n"@en.
:Analyze rdf:type owl:Class, gufo:SubKind, owl:NamedIndividual;
    rdfs:label "Analyze"@en;
    rdfs:comment "Analyze: breaking material into its constituent parts and determining how the parts are related to one another as well as to an overall structure or purpose.\n\n- Differentiating, discriminating, distinguishing, focusing, selecting: distinguishing relevant from irrelevant parts or important from unimportant parts of presented material.\n\n- Organizing, finding coherence, integrating, outlining, parsing, structuring: determining how elements fit or function within a structure\n\n- Attributing, deconstructing: determine a point of view, bias, values, or intent underlying presented material.\n"@en.
:Understand rdf:type owl:Class, gufo:SubKind, owl:NamedIndividual;
    rdfs:label "Understand"@en;
    rdfs:comment "Understand: constructing the meaning of instructional messages, including oral, written, and graphic communication. Build connections between the \"new\" knowledge to be gained and their prior knowledge.\n\n- Interpreting, clarifying, paraphrasing, representing, translating: changing from one form of representation to another.\n\n- Exemplifying, illustrating, instantiating: finding a specific example or illustration of a concept or principle.\n\n- Classifying, categorizing, subsuming: determining that something belongs to a category.\n\n- Summarizing, abstracting, generalizing.\n\n- Inferring, concluding, extrapolating, interpolating, predicting: recognize some pattern in the information given.\n\n- Comparing, contrasting, mapping, matching: detecting similarities and differences between two or more objects, events, ideas, problems, or situations.\n\n- Explaining, constructing models - search for causality in that pattern, construct and use a cause-and effect model of a system (Reasoning, troubleshooting, redesigning, and predicting).\n"@en.
:Create rdf:type owl:Class, gufo:SubKind, owl:NamedIndividual;
    rdfs:label "Create"@en;
    rdfs:comment "Create: putting elements together to form a novel, coherent whole or to make an original product.\n\n- Generating, hypothesizing: coming up with alternative hypotheses based on criteria.\n\n- Planning, designing.\n\n- Producing, constructing: inventing a product.\n"@en.
:Remember rdf:type owl:Class, gufo:SubKind, owl:NamedIndividual;
    rdfs:label "Remember"@en;
    rdfs:comment "Remember - retrieve relevant knowledge from long-term memory.\n\n- Recognizing, Identifying: e.g., recognize important dates, compare informations.\n\n- Recalling, Retrieving: e.g., recall important dates.\n"@en.
:Evaluate rdf:type owl:Class, gufo:SubKind, owl:NamedIndividual;
    rdfs:label "Evaluate"@en;
    rdfs:comment "Evaluate: making judgments based on criteria and/or standards. \n\n- Checking, coordinating, detecting, monitoring, testing: internal consistency, fallacies.\n\n- Critiquing, judging: consistency with external criteria; detecting the appropriateness of a procedure for a given problem.\n"@en.
:Competence rdf:type owl:Class, gufo:Kind, owl:NamedIndividual;
    rdfs:subClassOf gufo:FunctionalComplex;
    rdfs:label "Competence"@en;
    rdfs:comment "A competency specification enumerates knowledge, skills, and dispositions that are observable in the accomplishment of a task, a task that prescribes purpose within a work context.\n\nA competence is defined as the ability to mobilize and integrate knowledge, skills, attitudes, and values in real and varied contexts to perform activities or solve problems effectively and ethically. This definition encompasses several components:\n\nKnowledge: Theoretical and practical information that an individual possesses about a specific topic or area of study.\n\nSkills: Practical and technical abilities that enable an individual to apply their knowledge in concrete situations.\n\nAttitudes and Values: Emotional and behavioral dispositions that influence how an individual behaves and interacts with others, including aspects such as ethics, responsibility, empathy, and teamwork.\n\nContext: The specific environment or situation where the competence is applied, which can range from an academic setting to a professional or everyday environment.\n\nCompetency-based education focuses on the development and assessment of these competencies in an integrated and contextualized manner, ensuring that students not only acquire theoretical knowledge but also know how to apply it practically and relevantly in real situations. This approach promotes continuous and adaptive learning, preparing individuals to face constant challenges and changes in the modern world."@en.
:Task rdf:type owl:Class, gufo:Kind, owl:NamedIndividual;
    rdfs:subClassOf gufo:FunctionalComplex;
    rdfs:label "Task"@en;
    rdfs:comment "Task is the construct that frames the skilled application of knowledge and makes dispositions concrete.\n\nTask expressed as a colloquial prose statement provides the setting to manifest dispositions, where individuals moderate their choices, actions, and effort necessary to pursue and succeed in an efficient and effective manner. In this sense, task enfolds the\npurposeful context of competency, exposing the integral nature of knowledge, skills, and dispositions."@en.
:ComputingKnowledge rdf:type owl:Class, gufo:SubKind, owl:NamedIndividual;
    rdfs:label "ComputingKnowledge"@en.
:CompetenceACMModel rdf:type owl:Class, gufo:SubKind, owl:NamedIndividual;
    rdfs:label "CompetenceACMModel"@en;
    rdfs:comment "An effective specification of ACM Competency is a synthesis of (1) a colloquial, prose competency statement that sets out a task and (2) the component structure of constituent K, S, and D elements necessary to succeed in that task.\n\nA competency statement is a natural language expression of the competency that is more approachable and understandable to the general audience of curricula, while the more explicitly expressed component structure facilitates audit and analysis."@en.
:Context rdf:type owl:Class, gufo:SituationType, owl:NamedIndividual;
    rdfs:subClassOf gufo:Situation;
    rdfs:label "Context"@en.
:FoundationalProfessionalKnowledge rdf:type owl:Class, gufo:SubKind, owl:NamedIndividual;
    rdfs:label "FoundationalProfessionalKnowledge"@en.
:CompetenceEvidence rdf:type owl:Class, gufo:Kind, owl:NamedIndividual;
    rdfs:subClassOf gufo:FunctionalComplex;
    rdfs:label "CompetenceEvidence"@en.
:Actor rdf:type owl:Class, gufo:Kind, owl:NamedIndividual;
    rdfs:subClassOf gufo:FunctionalComplex;
    rdfs:label "Actor"@en.
:ProficiencyLevel rdf:type owl:Class, gufo:Kind, owl:NamedIndividual;
    rdfs:subClassOf gufo:FunctionalComplex;
    rdfs:label "ProficiencyLevel"@en.
:ProficiencyScale rdf:type owl:Class, gufo:Kind, owl:NamedIndividual;
    rdfs:subClassOf gufo:FunctionalComplex;
    rdfs:label "ProficiencyScale"@en.
:Assessment rdf:type owl:Class, gufo:Kind, owl:NamedIndividual;
    rdfs:subClassOf gufo:Relator;
    rdfs:label "Assessment"@en.
:EducationalResource rdf:type owl:Class, gufo:SubKind, owl:NamedIndividual;
    rdfs:label "EducationalResource"@en.
:Activity rdf:type owl:Class, gufo:SubKind, owl:NamedIndividual;
    rdfs:label "Activity"@en.
:Attitude rdf:type owl:Class, gufo:SubKind, owl:NamedIndividual;
    rdfs:label "Attitude"@en.
:LongIdentifierType rdf:type owl:Class, gufo:Kind, owl:NamedIndividual;
    rdfs:subClassOf gufo:FunctionalComplex;
    rdfs:label "LongIdentifierType"@en;
    rdfs:comment "This data type is an identifier (a label) that is intended to be unique among all contexts in which the identifier may be used. \n\nThe Catalog element is the name or designator of the identification or cataloging\nscheme for this entry, in other words, a namespace-scheme. \n\nThe Entry element is the value of the identifier within the identification or cataloging scheme that designates or identifies this RCD, in other words, a namespace-specific string.\n\nThe catalog and entry values may be concatenated as a single character string in an\napplication profile or binding. If the catalog and entry values are concatenated, the resulting character string shall be a globally unique identifier in the form of a URI. The identifier shall be unique across all systems that store or process the RCD. The total length of a concatenated identifier shall be no longer than 4000 characters."@en.
:LangString rdf:type owl:Class, gufo:Kind, owl:NamedIndividual;
    rdfs:subClassOf gufo:FunctionalComplex;
    rdfs:label "LangString"@en;
    rdfs:comment "This data type consists of a language specification for a localized string and the string itself.\n\nA LangString value may include multiple, semantically equivalent, character strings, such as translations or expressions of a description in different languages. (IEEE Std 1484.20.1-2007 )\n\nExamples of localized strings: \"Information Technology\" in French and\n\"localization\" in British English:\n\t\"fr\", \"Technologies de l'information\"\n\t\"en-GB\", \"localisation\""@en.
:Vocabulary rdf:type owl:Class, gufo:Kind, owl:NamedIndividual;
    rdfs:subClassOf gufo:FunctionalComplex;
    rdfs:label "Vocabulary"@en.
:Statement rdf:type owl:Class, gufo:Kind, owl:NamedIndividual;
    rdfs:subClassOf gufo:FunctionalComplex;
    rdfs:label "Statement"@en;
    rdfs:comment "Each instance of this data element is a description of a single characteristic of a Definition element. At least one Statement element is mandatory if a Definition element exists. A record of type Statement shall contain one or more of the elements defined in Statement type. None of these elements is specifically required, but at least one shall be present.\n"@en.
:Definition rdf:type owl:Class, gufo:Kind, owl:NamedIndividual;
    rdfs:subClassOf gufo:FunctionalComplex;
    rdfs:label "Definition"@en;
    rdfs:comment "This data element provides a structure for including an arbitrary collection of statements that describe a competency. The author of an RCD is free to use this data element in any way that best describes the competency."@en.
:Metadata rdf:type owl:Class, gufo:Kind, owl:NamedIndividual;
    rdfs:subClassOf gufo:FunctionalComplex;
    rdfs:label "Metadata"@en.
:ComposedCompetence rdfs:subClassOf :CompetenceACMModel.
:AtomicCompetence rdfs:subClassOf :CompetenceACMModel.
:Remember rdfs:subClassOf :BloomCognitiveSkill.
:Understand rdfs:subClassOf :Remember.
:Apply rdfs:subClassOf :Understand.
:Analyze rdfs:subClassOf :Apply.
:Evaluate rdfs:subClassOf :Analyze.
:Create rdfs:subClassOf :Evaluate.
:BloomCognitiveSkill rdfs:subClassOf :Skill.
:CompetenceACMModel rdfs:subClassOf :Competence.
:FoundationalProfessionalKnowledge rdfs:subClassOf :Knowledge.
:ComputingKnowledge rdfs:subClassOf :Knowledge.
:EducationalResource rdfs:subClassOf :Task.
:Activity rdfs:subClassOf :Task.
:Attitude rdfs:subClassOf :Disposition.
[ rdf:type owl:AllDisjointClasses ] owl:members (:AtomicCompetence :ComposedCompetence).
:litCatalog rdfs:domain :LongIdentifierType;
    rdfs:range xsd:string;
    rdf:type owl:DatatypeProperty;
    rdfs:subPropertyOf gufo:hasQualityValue;
    rdfs:label "litCatalog"@en.
:litEntry rdfs:domain :LongIdentifierType;
    rdfs:range xsd:string;
    rdf:type owl:DatatypeProperty;
    rdfs:subPropertyOf gufo:hasQualityValue;
    rdfs:label "litEntry"@en.
:langStringLanguage rdfs:domain :LangString;
    rdfs:range xsd:string;
    rdf:type owl:DatatypeProperty;
    rdfs:subPropertyOf gufo:hasQualityValue;
    rdfs:label "langStringLanguage"@en;
    rdfs:comment "This data element shall be a character string consisting of a required language code followed by multiple, optional, hyphen-prefixed subcodes.\n\nThe following constraints apply to the language code part of the character string:\n- Two-letter codes are defined by ISO 639-1.\n- Three-letter codes are defined by ISO 639-2.\n- The value prefix \"i\" is reserved for registrations defined by the Internet Assigned Numbers Authority (IANA).\n- The value prefix \"x\" is reserved for private use.\n\nThe following constraints apply to the first subcode part of the character string:\n- Two-letter subcodes are ISO 3166-1 alpha-2 country codes.\n- Subcodes of from three to eight letters are registered with IANA.\n\nConstraints for additional subcodes are unspecified.\nThe character string shall conform to the syntax defined for a valid language code by the XML Schema derived data type language (see W3C Recommendation XML Schema, Part 2 [28 October 2004]).\n\nISO 639-2 specifies two code sets, one for bibliographic applications (ISO 639-2/B) and one for terminology applications (ISO 639-2/T). Either code set may be used."@en.
:langStringString rdfs:domain :LangString;
    rdfs:range xsd:string;
    rdf:type owl:DatatypeProperty;
    rdfs:subPropertyOf gufo:hasQualityValue;
    rdfs:label "langStringString"@en.
:vocabularySource rdfs:domain :Vocabulary;
    rdfs:range xsd:string;
    rdf:type owl:DatatypeProperty;
    rdfs:subPropertyOf gufo:hasQualityValue;
    rdfs:label "vocabularySource"@en.
:vocabularyValue rdfs:domain :Vocabulary;
    rdfs:range xsd:string;
    rdf:type owl:DatatypeProperty;
    rdfs:subPropertyOf gufo:hasQualityValue;
    rdfs:label "vocabularyValue"@en.
:statementID rdfs:domain :Statement;
    rdfs:range :LongIdentifierType;
    rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty;
    rdfs:label "statementID"@en;
    rdfs:comment "This conditionally mandatory data element is a label for the statement. This label shall be unique at least within the scope of the definition.\n\nThe RCD Standard does not specify how IDs are created, assigned, or resolved."@en.
:statementName rdfs:domain :Statement;
    rdfs:range xsd:string;
    rdf:type owl:DatatypeProperty;
    rdfs:subPropertyOf gufo:hasQualityValue;
    rdfs:label "statementName"@en;
    rdfs:comment "This conditionally mandatory data element is a name for the statement. This name shall be unique at least within the scope of the definition. \n\nExamples: \"Condition\", \"Action\", \"Standard\", \"Outcome\", and \"Criteria\". The characters in the string shall belong to the repertoire of ISO/IEC 10646."@en.
:statementText rdfs:domain :Statement;
    rdfs:range :LangString;
    rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty;
    rdfs:label "statementText"@en;
    rdfs:comment "This conditionally mandatory data element is an unstructured, textual description of those aspects of the RCD referred to by the Statement name element.\n\nA competency free-form statement represents the competency in terms that are familiar and comprehensible to a wide audience, typically using a vocabulary familiar to, and that resonates with, the audience. The competency statement is then structurally augmented and amplified in the enumeration of knowledge, skills, and dispositions that complete the competency specification"@en.
:statementToken rdfs:domain :Statement;
    rdfs:range :Vocabulary;
    rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty;
    rdfs:label "statementToken"@en;
    rdfs:comment "This conditionally mandatory data element consists of a vocabulary token, along with an identifier of the token�s source, which allows the use of controlled terms (vocabularies) instead of, or along with, free-form statement text.\n\nA record of type vocabulary shall contain both a Source element and a Value element. \n\nThe Source element indicates the source of the token value. The Source element may be a URI that identifies a formal vocabulary definition. "@en.
:definitionModelSource rdfs:domain :Definition;
    rdfs:range xsd:string;
    rdf:type owl:DatatypeProperty;
    rdfs:subPropertyOf gufo:hasQualityValue;
    rdfs:label "definitionModelSource"@en;
    rdfs:comment "This optional data element is the source of the model used for the competency definition.\n\nThe value of this data element should be sufficiently specific to avoid conflict with other source names; therefore, the value should be a Uniform Resource Identifier (URI). If the value of this data element is a URI, it may point to an actual document that defines the source formally.\n\nTypically, the models that underlie this data element define competencies in terms of \"statement, conditions, criteria,\" \"proficiency, criteria, indicators,\" \"standards, performance indicators, outcomes,\" \"abilities, basic skills, content, process,\" and similar sets of statements."@en.
:definitionStatement rdfs:domain :Definition;
    rdfs:range :Statement;
    rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty;
    rdfs:label "definitionStatement"@en;
    rdfs:comment "Each instance of this data element is a description of a single characteristic of a Definition element. At least one Statement element is mandatory if a Definition element exists. \n\nA record of type Statement shall contain one or more of the elements defined in statement type. None of these elements is specifically required, but at least one shall be present."@en.
:rcdSchema rdfs:domain :Metadata;
    rdfs:range xsd:string;
    rdf:type owl:DatatypeProperty;
    rdfs:subPropertyOf gufo:hasQualityValue;
    rdfs:label "rcdSchema"@en;
    rdfs:comment "This optional data element is a label for the schema that defines and controls the RCD instance. If this data element is omitted from the RCD instance, then a value of  \"ieee.org/1484.20.1/2007\" shall be assumed."@en.
:rcdSchemaVersion rdfs:domain :Metadata;
    rdfs:range xsd:string;
    rdf:type owl:DatatypeProperty;
    rdfs:subPropertyOf gufo:hasQualityValue;
    rdfs:label "rcdSchemaVersion"@en;
    rdfs:comment "This optional data element describes the version of the schema described by the RCD schema element.\nIf this element is omitted, then a value of  \"1.0\" shall be assumed."@en.
:additionalMetadata rdfs:domain :Metadata;
    rdfs:range :void;
    rdf:type owl:DatatypeProperty;
    rdfs:subPropertyOf gufo:hasQualityValue;
    rdfs:label "additionalMetadata"@en;
    rdfs:comment "Zero or more instances of additional, embedded metadata that describe the RCD instance may be included in the metadata element. If additional metadata are present, the actual type of each element shall be defined by an application profile. \nAn implementation shall accept any additional metadata record, but it is not required to interpret such additional metadata records."@en.
:dispositionName rdfs:domain :Disposition;
    rdfs:range :LangString;
    rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty;
    rdfs:label "dispositionName"@en.
:skillLevel rdfs:domain :Skill;
    rdfs:range xsd:int;
    rdf:type owl:DatatypeProperty;
    rdfs:subPropertyOf gufo:hasQualityValue;
    rdfs:label "skillLevel"@en.
:skillDescription rdfs:domain :Skill;
    rdfs:range :LangString;
    rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty;
    rdfs:label "skillDescription"@en;
    rdfs:comment "Nome atribu�do a cada n�vel da Taxonomia."@en.
:skillName rdfs:domain :Skill;
    rdfs:range :LangString;
    rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty;
    rdfs:label "skillName"@en.
:knowledgeIRI rdfs:domain :Knowledge;
    rdfs:range :LongIdentifierType;
    rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty;
    rdfs:label "knowledgeIRI"@en.
:knowledgeName rdfs:domain :Knowledge;
    rdfs:range :LangString;
    rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty;
    rdfs:label "knowledgeName"@en.
:verbName rdfs:domain :CognitiveActionVerbs;
    rdfs:range xsd:string;
    rdf:type owl:DatatypeProperty;
    rdfs:subPropertyOf gufo:hasQualityValue;
	rdfs:label "Verb Name"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "The name of the cognitive action verb." .
:verbDescription rdfs:domain :CognitiveActionVerbs;
    rdfs:range xsd:string;
    rdf:type owl:DatatypeProperty;
    rdfs:subPropertyOf gufo:hasQualityValue;
    rdfs:label "Verb Description"@en;
    rdfs:comment "A description of the cognitive action verb." .	
:hasIdentifier rdfs:domain :Competence;
    rdfs:range :LongIdentifierType;
    rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty;
    rdfs:label "hasIdentifier"@en;
    rdfs:comment "This mandatory data element is a globally unique label that identifies the RCD (Reusable Competency Definition). \nThe label shall be unique across all systems that store or process the RCD."@en.
:hasTitle rdfs:domain :Competence;
    rdfs:range :LangString;
    rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty;
    rdfs:label "hasTitle"@en;
    rdfs:comment "This mandatory data element is a single, text label for the RCD. \nThe label is a human-readable name for the RCD. \n\nBecause different application domains may coincidentally define the same title for different competency definitions, the Identifier element, not the Title, shall be used to distinguish among RCDs. \n\nThe Title element is intended to provide a title, only, and should not be used to provide descriptive detail in lieu of the Description element."@en.
:hasDescription rdfs:domain :Competence;
    rdfs:range :LangString;
    rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty;
    rdfs:label "hasDescription"@en;
    rdfs:comment "This optinal data element is a human-readable description of the competency. It is an unstructured character string meant to be interpretable only by humans."@en.
:hasDefinition rdfs:domain :Competence;
    rdfs:range :Definition;
    rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty;
    rdfs:label "hasDefinition"@en;
    rdfs:comment "This data element is a structured description that provides a formal definition of the competency,usually using attributes taken from a specific model of how a competency should be structured or defined.\n\nThis data element shall contain zero or one Model source elements and at least one Statement element. It may contain multiple Statement elements."@en.
:hasMetadata rdfs:domain :Competence;
    rdfs:range :Metadata;
    rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty;
    rdfs:label "hasMetadata"@en;
    rdfs:comment "This optional data element consists of embedded metadata about the RCD. This data element does not preclude the use of external metadata about the RCD. Such external metadata are not defined by RCD Standard."@en.
:taskDescription rdfs:domain :Task;
    rdfs:range :LangString;
    rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty;
    rdfs:label "taskDescription"@en.
:contextDescription rdfs:domain :Context;
    rdfs:range :LangString;
    rdf:type owl:ObjectProperty;
    rdfs:label "contextDescription"@en.
:minValue rdfs:domain :ProficiencyScale;
    rdfs:range xsd:int;
    rdf:type owl:DatatypeProperty;
    rdfs:subPropertyOf gufo:hasQualityValue;
    rdfs:label "minValue"@en.
:maxValue rdfs:domain :ProficiencyScale;
    rdfs:range xsd:int;
    rdf:type owl:DatatypeProperty;
    rdfs:subPropertyOf gufo:hasQualityValue;
    rdfs:label "maxValue"@en.
:description rdfs:domain :ProficiencyScale;
    rdfs:range xsd:string;
    rdf:type owl:DatatypeProperty;
    rdfs:subPropertyOf gufo:hasQualityValue;
    rdfs:label "description"@en.
:assessmentDate rdfs:domain :Assessment;
    rdfs:range xsd:string;
    rdf:type owl:DatatypeProperty;
    rdfs:subPropertyOf gufo:hasQualityValue;
    rdfs:label "assessmentDate"@en.
